```json
{
  "root_causes": [
    {
      "service": "geo",
      "fault_kind": "pod_failure",
      "evidence": [
        {"kind": "metric", "sql": "SELECT attr_k8s_pod_name pod, metric, COUNT(*) n, MIN(value) mn, MAX(value) mx FROM abnormal_metrics WHERE attr_k8s_pod_name LIKE 'geo%' AND metric='k8s.container.restarts' GROUP BY 1,2", "claim": "geo pod restart counter rises from 2 to 6 during the incident; it was 0 in the normal period."},
        {"kind": "metric", "sql": "SELECT 'normal' p, metric, attr_k8s_deployment_name dep, AVG(value) v FROM normal_metrics WHERE metric='k8s.deployment.available' AND attr_k8s_deployment_name='geo' GROUP BY 1,2,3 UNION ALL SELECT 'abnormal', metric, attr_k8s_deployment_name, AVG(value) FROM abnormal_metrics WHERE metric='k8s.deployment.available' AND attr_k8s_deployment_name='geo' GROUP BY 1,2,3", "claim": "geo deployment availability drops from 1.0 in normal to 0.0 in the incident period."},
        {"kind": "trace", "sql": "SELECT service_name, span_name, attr_span_kind, COUNT(*) n FROM abnormal_traces WHERE service_name='geo' GROUP BY 1,2,3 UNION ALL SELECT 'normal_geo', span_name, attr_span_kind, COUNT(*) FROM normal_traces WHERE service_name='geo' GROUP BY 1,2,3", "claim": "geo emits no server spans during the incident, versus 285 in the normal period."},
        {"kind": "trace", "sql": "SELECT service_name, span_name, attr_status_code, COUNT(*) n, ROUND(AVG(duration)/1e6,2) avg_ms FROM abnormal_traces WHERE span_name='geo.Geo/Nearby' AND attr_span_kind='Client' GROUP BY 1,2,3", "claim": "search's geo.Geo/Nearby client calls all return Error in about 0.02 ms, so they fail immediately."}
      ]
    }
  ],
  "propagation": [
    {"from": "geo", "to": "search",
     "evidence": [{"kind": "trace", "sql": "SELECT service_name, span_name, attr_status_code, COUNT(*) n FROM abnormal_traces WHERE service_name='search' AND span_name IN ('geo.Geo/Nearby','search.Search/Nearby') GROUP BY 1,2,3", "claim": "search's geo calls all fail with Error status and its own Search/Nearby spans are all Error."},
                  {"kind": "log", "sql": "SELECT service_name, message, COUNT(*) n FROM abnormal_logs WHERE service_name='search' AND level='ERROR' GROUP BY 1,2", "claim": "search logs 'Failed to get nearby hotels from geo service' about 1,595 times."}]},
    {"from": "search", "to": "frontend",
     "evidence": [{"kind": "trace", "sql": "SELECT service_name, span_name, attr_http_response_status_code, attr_status_code, COUNT(*) n FROM abnormal_traces WHERE service_name='frontend' AND span_name='HTTP /hotels' GROUP BY 1,2,3,4", "claim": "frontend's HTTP /hotels returns 500 with Error status on 461 of 463 requests."}]}
  ]
}
```

**Summary:** The user-visible degradation is on frontend `/hotels`, which returns HTTP 500 for nearly every request. The `search` service calls `geo` and fails on every call. `geo` is crash-looping: its restart counter climbs during the incident and its deployment shows zero available replicas. Its server spans disappear entirely. Search's CPU also climbs to about 2.8 cores and its error-log volume grows roughly tenfold per request. I read that as a symptom of the retry/error loop against geo, not a separate fault, so I did not name search as a root cause. The drop in calls to `rate`, `profile` and `reservation` follows from search failing before it reaches them. Frontend's `/hotels` latency and volume are the user-facing impact.
